        }
      ],
      "why": "iterative is the baseline shared-datapath family with the smallest structural overhead (one barrel shifter/adder tree, no k-way or m-way replication). Since the spec is 'area is everything' and throughput only needs >= 1 MSPS (which iterative can meet if Fmax is high enough, or even if Fmax is modest since it needs 1 result per cycle at 1 MSPS -> Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially met), iterative is the prime candidate for the minimum luts_plus_ffs corner. I give it the largest share to densely map the accuracy-vs-area trade-off (n_iter, data_width, guards) that defines the Pareto front."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 30,
          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "k"
        }
      ],
      "why": "unrolled_k trades area for latency/throughput by chaining k micro-rotations per cycle. While it generally uses more LUTs/FFs than iterative for the same accuracy (due to k parallel datapaths or deeper combinational logic), it can achieve higher Fmax for a given n_iter by reducing the per-cycle combinational depth, which may help if iterative's Fmax is too low to meet 1 MSPS at a given area. More importantly, for the hypervolume objective (covering the trade-off curve), unrolled_k may occupy a different region of the (luts_plus_ffs, accuracy_bits) space, especially at higher k where the area penalty is offset by potentially higher Fmax enabling more aggressive n_iter within timing constraints. I give it a smaller share since iterative is more likely to dominate the minimum-area corner, but it is needed to ensure the front is well-mapped."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec prioritizes minimum area (luts_plus_ffs) with a hard throughput constraint of >= 1 MSPS and max error <= 2^-10. The iterative family is the most area-efficient baseline and is the most likely to produce the minimum-area design that meets the constraints, so it gets the largest budget share (55%) to densely explore the accuracy-area trade-off. The unrolled_k family (45%) is included to cover the trade-off curve more completely for hypervolume scoring, as it may achieve different (area, accuracy) points due to its different timing characteristics and structural overhead. Both families can plausibly meet the 1 MSPS throughput constraint (iterative needs Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially achievable; unrolled_k needs Fmax >= 1/k MHz, even easier). The full ranges for n_iter, data_width, and guards are used to let Optuna discover the Pareto front, with the accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-10) acting as a filter that will naturally focus the search on designs with sufficient n_iter and appropriate guard bits."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established and satisfies all constraints. The minimum area design (iterative, luts_plus_ffs=345) meets the throughput constraint (10.8 MSPS >= 1) and the accuracy constraint (13.8 bits > 10 bits). The front spans a wide range of accuracy (13.8 to 23.4 bits) and area (345 to 1254), providing a comprehensive trade-off curve. Since this is the final round, no further exploration is possible or necessary. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) will pick the iterative design with 345 LUTs+FFs, which is feasible and optimal for the given objectives."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
